            <h2>Recreation and Tourism – City of Green River.</h2>
            <p>Over the last <span data-field="time_period">{{ time_period }}</span> through its comprehensive planning process, the city of <span data-field="city_name">{{ city_name }}</span>, has invested heavily in its parks and recreational trails system as well as development of a whitewater facility on the <span data-field="river_name">{{ river_name }}</span> at <span data-field="location_detail">{{ location_detail }}</span>. Green River was one of only <span data-field="number_of_communities">{{ number_of_communities }}</span> in the United States to receive a national Playful City USA Award in <span data-field="year_awarded">{{ year_awarded }}</span>, an award it also won in <span data-field="year_awarded_2">{{ year_awarded_2 }}</span>. Green River’s commitment to outdoor recreation planning promotes wellness among its citizens and fosters tourism and economic development in the community.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="column">
            <p>for housing. The goal of the policy is to help maintain the open character and agricultural viability of the county’s rural areas, continue the use of lands for farm and ranch activities, conserve riparian areas and other natural resources such as wildlife habitat, and avoid placing residential buildings in floodplains or on steep slopes. As a result of these planning efforts, the <span data-field="planning_body">{{ planning_body }}</span> awarded Sheridan County with the Rural Planning Project of the Year award in <span data-field="year_award">{{ year_award }}</span>.</p>
            <img src="placeholder_image_2.jpg" alt="Landscape image" style="width: 100%; height: auto; margin-top: 20px;">
            <h3>Coordinated Energy Development – Carbon County.</h3>
            <p>Almost <span data-field="percentage_energy">{{ percentage_energy }}</span> of the land in Carbon County, Wyoming, is owned by the federal government. Similar to many of Wyoming’s other 22 counties, Carbon County has chosen to be an active partner in federal land management activities, including planning for oil, gas, and wind energy development. Carbon County has accomplished this in part by incorporating goals related to multiple use development opportunities in its county-wide comprehensive land-use plan. In doing so, the county has established a policy tool that can be used to advocate for local government consultation and coordination in federal agency land management decisions.</p>
