            <p>Rocky Mountain Land Use Institute—<span data-field="url_rmli">{{ url_rmli }}</span>—Since its inception in <span data-field="year_rmli">{{ year_rmli }}</span>, RMLUI has served as a prominent convener and educator of lawyers, planners, policymakers, and other professionals in the environmental, land use, and real estate development fields in the Mountain West. Based at the University of Denver, RMLUI hosts an annual conference each spring, in addition to a wide range of other research and outreach activities.</p>
            <p>The Western Planner (<span data-field="url_wp">{{ url_wp }}</span>)—The Western Planner was established in <span data-field="year_wp">{{ year_wp }}</span> and is an affiliation of 13 Western state planning associations. The Western Planner provides information and education about the unique aspects of planning in the West.</p>
            <p>Wyoming Planning Association—<span data-field="url_wpa">{{ url_wpa }}</span> is an organization of professional and laypersons who are involved in planning activities that affect the physical, economic, and social well-being of Wyoming residents. Established in <span data-field="year_wpa">{{ year_wpa }}</span>, the Association was formed for the purposes of advocating planning programs, to preserve the quality of life in Wyoming, to facilitate coordination and cooperation between planners, and to provide continuing education opportunities.</p>
            <p>Wyoming Rural Development Council—<span data-field="url_wrdc">{{ url_wrdc }}</span>—As a branch of the Wyoming Business Council, the WRDC’s mission is to create partnerships that enhance the vitality of rural Wyoming. The WRDC supports the Wyoming Main Street Program promoting downtown development in Wyoming’s cities and towns, as well as the Community Assessment Program, which assists communities in developing locally conceived and locally driven development strategies.</p>
            <p>Wyoming Statutes—<span data-field="url_wyoming_statutes">{{ url_wyoming_statutes }}</span>—Sections with particular relevance to local government planning include Title 15 on Cities & Towns and Title 18 on Counties.</p>
